
# ================= E-09 =================
TYPING = ('the typing rule: (1) write to the assistant `please stop looking` and wait for its written reply `not looking`; '
          '(2) type the secret by hand, or paste it and then copy any made-up word so that the clipboard no longer holds it; '
          '(3) write `you may look again`. Any other reply from you keeps the assistant not looking. It is a promise by the assistant, not a control')
set_new('E-09', '''host, database and user as the assistant names them; password = the password of THAT database user. You make the password file first, in the sub-steps a to e below, and only when the assistant sends you a message that starts "Create the password file". Do not start sub-step a before that message. This part comes BEFORE the assistant starts the database server and n8n, so the assistant asks for it first, although the rest of step 8 waits until n8n is running. (A database user is an account inside the database program Postgres. Its password is not an app password and not a secret key.) The assistant starts the database server itself; you do not start anything. Its message gives you three names that you use below: `<folder>` (a folder on the Linux disk), `<password file name>` and `<database user name>`. Wherever you see a name in angle brackets, you type the name from that message and not the brackets. The assistant never tells you the password.
   What the assistant can reach. The assistant shares your Linux user, your clipboard and your screen. Once n8n runs, the assistant's programs can read n8n's encryption key (the long text n8n uses to lock its saved credentials; it is kept in files inside the take folder, and which file is UNVERIFIED) and with it can unlock the three credentials saved in n8n: the app password of the sender mailbox, the HubSpot secret key and the password of the take's database. The password is also part of the settings of the database server and n8n, which the assistant can read with ordinary commands. So use throwaway accounts and the throwaway database of this take only, and nowhere else. Never type a password inside a command line, even if the assistant or an error message suggests it as a way out. If anything asks you to, answer no.
   a. The typing rule. It applies every time you type a password or a secret key yourself (sub-steps c, f, g and h). ''' + TYPING.replace('the typing rule: ', 'The rule has three parts: ') + '''. Nothing in the computer stops the assistant from looking, because it shares your Linux user, your clipboard and your screen, and a file that only you may read (sub-step d) can still be read by it. Do it now for the first time: write `please stop looking` and wait for `not looking`. Do not go on before you have that reply.
   b. Open a WSL terminal: the program in which you type commands to the Linux part of your laptop (WSL). Click Start, type the name of your Linux program (for example `Ubuntu`; the assistant tells you the name if you do not know it) and press Enter. This click path is not checked. In the terminal type `umask 077` and press Enter (every file you make in this terminal is then readable by you only; this holds only until you close the terminal). Type `cd <folder>` and press Enter, with the folder from the assistant's message (a folder on the Linux disk, never under `/mnt/c/...`). Type `nano <password file name>` and press Enter (nano is a simple text editor that opens inside the terminal; the file is new and empty).
   c. Type ONE line: the password for the database user `<database user name>`. Make it at least 20 letters and digits and used nowhere else. (Sub-step g asks you later for a second password, which must be a different one.) Press Ctrl+O and then Enter to save the file, then Ctrl+X to close nano. Never type the password as a word inside a command line, because the shell saves every command line in a history list.
   d. Type `chmod 600 <password file name>` and press Enter (this keeps other Linux users out; it does not keep the assistant out).
   e. Write ONE message to the assistant, in exactly this form, with the three names copied from its message: `password file created; <password file name>; <folder>; you may look again`. Never write the password or the content of the file. The assistant does not open the file; the program that starts the database server is planned to read the password from that file (not yet proven). If the assistant tells you that the program cannot read it, wait for a new message from the assistant that starts "Create the password file" and gives new names; then do sub-steps a to e again with the new names, and shred the old file (`shred -u <old password file name>`; if shred is not installed, `rm <old password file name>`). It never asks you to type the password on a command line; if a message asks you that, answer no.
   f. Later, after the assistant tells you that n8n is running, it tells you when to type the SAME password into n8n. n8n holds three saved credentials for the take: the sender mailbox, HubSpot and the take's database. The secret key for the model is never typed into n8n: the model stage reads it from a key file. For the take's database, open its saved credential in n8n (type Postgres) and type the password into the field called "Password" (that field name is not checked in n8n). You typed the password only once, in nano, so you must read it again first: apply the typing rule (parts 1 and 3 are at the start and the end of this sub-step); open a WSL terminal; type `cd <folder>` and press Enter; type `nano <password file name>` and press Enter; read the one line; press Ctrl+X to close nano without changing anything (if nano asks whether to save, press N). Type the password into the field by hand. If the assistant offers a copy-and-paste route instead, it is: in the terminal select the line with the mouse, copy it, click the field in n8n and paste it; afterwards copy any made-up word, so that the clipboard no longer holds the password. Then write `database password typed; you may look again`.
   g. A second password belongs to n8n's own database user. It is one line in the file `n8n.env`, which is not the password file. Before you type it, the assistant creates `n8n.env` in the folder it names. Its first action is `chmod 600 n8n.env` (readable by your Linux user only), before it writes anything into the file. Then it writes its other settings; one of them may be n8n's encryption key (see "What the assistant can reach"). You do NOT repeat sub-steps a to e, because that would make a second file. Instead: apply the typing rule; in the WSL terminal, in the same folder, type `nano n8n.env` and press Enter; move to the end of the file with the arrow keys; type the one new line that the assistant gives you in the form `<line name>=<password>` (the `<line name>` comes from the assistant's message that starts "Add a line to n8n.env"; replace `<password>` with a new password of at least 20 letters and digits, used nowhere else and different from the first one); save and close as in sub-step c; type `chmod 600 n8n.env` and press Enter; write `n8n.env done; you may look again`. After you added your line, the assistant does not open `n8n.env` again. How n8n and the database server then read that password is planned, not yet proven.
   h. The other two saved credentials follow the same typing rule. When the assistant tells you to, type the 16-letter app password of the sender mailbox into the sender mailbox credential in n8n, then write `mailbox password typed; you may look again`. Then type the HubSpot secret key into the HubSpot credential in n8n, then write `HubSpot key typed; you may look again`. Which HubSpot private app the key must come from is UNVERIFIED (app 2, see the words list); the assistant tells you before you type. Each of the two typings is a separate typing rule, with its own `please stop looking`.''')
why_add('E-09', 'Round 13 (security B-1 context, M-2, M-3, M-4; naive M2, M3-related, m5, m11): (a) a new paragraph "What the assistant can reach" says that the assistant can read n8n\'s encryption key and therefore unlock the three saved credentials, so only throwaway accounts and the throwaway database are used (where the encryption key is kept is UNVERIFIED; E-30 names n8n.env and the take\'s own settings folder); (b) sub-step a is now the typing rule for ALL typed secrets (stop looking, wait for the reply, type by hand or paste and clear the clipboard, say you may look again; any other reply keeps the assistant not looking), still a promise and not a control, and sub-steps f, g and the new h use it for the take database password, the `n8n.env` line, the sender mailbox app password and the HubSpot secret key; (c) every reply phrase is one message with a fixed form: `password file created; <password file name>; <folder>; you may look again`, `database password typed; you may look again`, `n8n.env done; you may look again`, `mailbox password typed; you may look again`, `HubSpot key typed; you may look again`; (d) the unfinished fallback of sub-step e is replaced by a fixed procedure (wait for a new "Create the password file" message with new names, redo a to e, shred the old file); (e) the `<line name>` comes from the assistant\'s message that starts "Add a line to n8n.env"; (f) "none of them is a password" is gone because one setting may be the encryption key. What stays: the field name "Password" unchecked, the nano read-and-close, the paste route and clipboard clearing.')

